"""Publish a reproducible report from the real group-batching measurements."""
import json,statistics,html,shutil
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).parent
r=json.loads((ROOT/'batch64-result.json').read_text());extra=json.loads((ROOT/'batch64-nine-result.json').read_text())
assert r['completed'] and extra['completed']
def median(rows):return statistics.median(x['inference_ms'] for x in rows)
def table(headers,rows):
 return '<div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+html.escape(str(x))+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
def sample(b,data=r):return [x for x in data['rows'] if x['label']=='timing' and x['groups_per_batch']==b]
def summary_rows(data,bs):
 base=median(sample(1,data));out=[]
 for b in bs:
  rows=sample(b,data);assert all('error' not in x for x in rows)
  out.append([b,len(rows),len(rows[0]['forward_shapes']),f'{median(rows)/1000:.3f} s',f'{min(x["inference_ms"] for x in rows)/1000:.3f}–{max(x["inference_ms"] for x in rows)/1000:.3f} s',f'{100*(1-median(rows)/base):.1f}%',f'{max(x["peak_allocated_mb"] for x in rows)/1024:.2f} GiB'])
 return out
base=median(sample(1));best=median(sample(8));savings=100*(1-best/base)
accuracy=[]
for b in [1,2,4,8]:
 rows=sample(b)
 accuracy.append([b,f'{max(x["score_difference"]["max_abs"] for x in rows):.6f}',f'{statistics.mean(x["score_difference"]["mean_abs"] for x in rows):.6f}',max(x['score_difference']['flips_at_0_5'] for x in rows),f'{min(x["score_difference"]["top10_overlap"] for x in rows)}–{max(x["score_difference"]["top10_overlap"] for x in rows)} / 10'])
native=next(x for x in r['rows'] if x['label']=='native_comparison');dedup=next(x for x in r['rows'] if x['label']=='dedup_comparison');repeat=next(x for x in r['rows'] if x['label']=='repeat_comparison')
headers=['Groups / batch','Warm runs','Actual forwards','Median','Range','Time saved vs 1','Peak allocated tensors']
body=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>d1 Audio Decision · 64-class group batching</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="./style.css"><style>main{{max-width:1140px}}h1{{font-size:44px}}h2{{color:var(--ink);font-size:22px;margin:15px 0;letter-spacing:-.5px}}p,li{{line-height:1.7}}.table-scroll{{overflow:auto}}table{{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px;margin:18px 0}}th,td{{padding:12px 10px;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line)}}th{{background:#e7efe2}}code{{overflow-wrap:anywhere;font-size:12px}}.note{{border-left:4px solid #bf7c32;padding:14px;background:#fff3df;border-radius:8px}}</style></head><body><header><a class="brand" href="./">ROBBY <span>RESEARCH</span></a><a href="./report.html">Original measurements ↗</a></header><main><div style="padding:40px 0 20px"><p class="eyebrow">9 NOVEMBER 2026 · REAL GPU EXPERIMENT</p><h1>Batch the groups,<br><em>keep the questions.</em></h1><p>527 AudioSet classes, ten seconds of audio history, a 200 ms target, BF16. Original 64-class multi-label groups remain unchanged. Only the number of independent groups processed together changes.</p></div>
<section class="panel"><h2>Result</h2><p>Eight groups per GPU batch reduced median window inference from <strong>{base/1000:.3f} s to {best/1000:.3f} s</strong>: <strong>{savings:.1f}% less time</strong>, or {base/best:.2f}× throughput. Two groups already capture most of the benefit and require less memory. Four groups did not improve the median over two in this small run.</p><p>A separate follow-up put all nine groups into <strong>one verified model forward</strong> and measured <strong>{median(sample(9,extra))/1000:.3f} s</strong>. This still falls well short of processing all 527 classes every 200 ms.</p><p class="note">These are isolated experimental paths, not a deployment change. The public demo still uses its previous sequential group loop. Batching does not solve the model’s previously observed time-localization errors.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Main comparison: 1, 2, 4 and 8 groups</h2><p>Three authored inputs: a final beep, chirp plus noise, and silence. Three warm repetitions per input/configuration: nine measurements per row. Orders rotate between repetitions. No microphone recordings or mock model outputs. RTX 3090 GPU 1; the machine and GPU remain shared with existing resident services. Ranges are observed minima/maxima, not confidence intervals.</p>{table(headers,summary_rows(r,[1,2,4,8]))}<p>“Peak allocated tensors” is PyTorch’s active peak for this process, including model weights. It excludes cached allocator reservations, CUDA-context overhead and other processes. A 30% per-process PyTorch memory cap limited this experiment to approximately 7.2 GiB of allocator memory. No out-of-memory errors occurred.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Follow-up: put the final group in the same batch</h2><p>There are eight full groups of 64 classes and one group of 15. This separate test uses the final-beep input and five warm repetitions per configuration, with its own sequential control. Compare rows within this table, rather than treating its timing differences from the main study as a model improvement.</p>{table(headers,summary_rows(extra,[1,8,9]))}<p>The runner actually executed row batches <code>[9]</code>, compared with <code>[8, 1]</code>. All 527 scores were returned and mapped back to their official IDs. This is nine independent sequences in one GPU forward, not a rewritten 527-choice question. The largest batch contained {max(s['padded_tokens'] for x in extra['rows'] for s in x['forward_shapes']):,} padded tokens.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Do the scores change?</h2><p>The new single-group path exactly matched the deployed implementation: maximum absolute score difference <strong>{r['implementation_vs_deployment']['max_abs']}</strong>. Different GPU batch shapes produce small numerical differences even with identical questions and media. The table compares against a sequential reference for the same input.</p>{table(['Groups / batch','Largest absolute difference','Average absolute difference','Max changes across 0.5','Top-10 overlap'],accuracy)}<p>The largest main-study difference was 0.006158 (about 0.62 percentage points on the 0–1 scale). No presence decision crossed 0.5 in these inputs. Near-tied classes sometimes exchanged positions around the Top-10 boundary. This is numerical consistency evidence, not proof of unchanged real-world recognition accuracy or probability calibration.</p><p>Repeated-target audio also worked at batch 8: {repeat['inference_ms']/1000:.3f} s in the single follow-up measurement, maximum score difference {repeat['score_difference']['max_abs']:.6f} from the corresponding sequential run.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>What was changed in the experiment?</h2><ol><li>Send several <code>(state, questions, media)</code> requests together through the documented <code>system_one_batch</code> interface. Keep each original 64-class multi-label question intact; the last group has 15 classes.</li><li>Raise the runner’s padded-token budget from the production limit of 4,096 to 65,536 for the bounded experiment. Otherwise a batch API call could still be internally split. Instrument <code>_slot_logits</code> to count the actual forward passes and their row lengths.</li><li>Reuse preprocessing for the identical audio/context. Verify the exact repeated mel tensor and mask pattern before reusing projected audio features across batch rows. Clip order is preserved, including the optional repeated target.</li><li>Preserve native independent scores. No softmax across AudioSet classes, no missing-class zero fill, no scene/prompt changes, and no cross-window reuse of incompatible features.</li></ol><p>A native batch-8 run without feature reuse took {native['inference_ms']/1000:.3f} s, compared with {dedup['inference_ms']/1000:.3f} s for the deduplicated run. This single pair is too small to claim a reliable standalone speedup. Deduplication encoded one clip instead of nine repeated copies; the maximum score difference from the native batched run was {dedup['score_difference']['max_abs']:.6f}. Reduced-precision kernels can depend on batch shape, so feature reuse is not claimed bit-identical to the native multi-clip encoding.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Warm-up, deployment and next steps</h2><p>Model loading took {r['load_ms']/1000:.2f} s and is excluded from the warm table. Each batch shape was exercised before timing. The experiment used the exact checkpoint <code>{r['revision']}</code>, PyTorch {r['software']['torch']}, BF16 and eager execution; <code>torch.compile</code> remained disabled.</p><p>Robby, TTS, ASR and the resident decision model on GPU 0 were not stopped or reconfigured. The test model used GPU 1, then exited and released its memory. Production timings and live latency have not been remeasured with this experimental batching path because it has not been installed in the service.</p><p>For integration, batch all groups when the token/VRAM budget permits, with smaller batches as a fallback. Batch 2 is an attractive low-memory choice; batch 8 or all 9 give the best measured throughput. First validate long scene descriptions, 20-second history, repeated targets and GPU contention. The current short-context tests do not establish those operating limits.</p></section>
<section class="panel"><h2>Reproduce and inspect</h2><p><a href="./batch64-result.json">Main results: every score, timing, memory peak and forward shape</a><br><a href="./batch64-nine-result.json">All-nine follow-up results</a><br><a href="./batch64-method.md">Commands, methodology and file locations</a></p><p>Source files: <code>audio_decision/batch64_study.py</code>, <code>batch64_nine.py</code>, <code>batch64_report.py</code>. They use the pinned official model API and run on real audio tensors. The synthetic inputs are authored test signals, not invented model responses.</p><p>Reference: <a href="https://huggingface.co/LiquidAI/d1-Omni-2B">official d1-Omni-2B model card and API</a>. The earlier <a href="./report.html">temporal-focus counterexample and live measurements</a> remain applicable.</p></section><footer><a href="./">Open the Audio Decision Lab ↗</a></footer></main></body></html>'''
(ROOT/'static/batch64.html').write_text(body)
for name in ['batch64-result.json','batch64-nine-result.json']:shutil.copyfile(ROOT/name,ROOT/'static'/name)
print('Published local report artifact: audio_decision/static/batch64.html')
